+<p><code>goto-last-change</code> visits the most recent edit in the current
+buffer and then progressively older edits when repeated.
+<code>goto-last-change-reverse</code> moves back toward more recent edits.
+Changes within eight buffer positions are treated as one location by default;
+a numeric prefix changes this span. A negative prefix also reverses the
+traversal direction. Reverse traversal is only available after starting a
+change-history navigation sequence. The navigation cache retains the 4096
+most recent changes and reports when older history has been truncated.</p>
+
 <hr>
 <a name="Region-handling_002c-kill-commands"></a>
 <table class="header" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1" border="0">
@@ -355,10 +367,27 @@ M-C-w                   : append-next-kill
 C-k                     : kill-line
 C-w                     : kill-region
 M-w                     : copy-region
+M-\                     : duplicate-dwim
+C-\                     : copy-from-above-command
 C-y                     : yank
 M-y                     : yank-pop
 </pre></div>
 
+<p>With no active region, <code>kill-region</code> and
+<code>copy-region</code> operate on the current line, or on the number of
+lines specified by a numeric prefix. Text copied this way is yanked at the
+beginning of the current line.</p>
+
+<p>Whole-line duplicate and yank operations add a terminating newline when the
+source final line does not have one, so the inserted text remains a complete
+line.</p>
+
+<p><code>duplicate-dwim</code> duplicates the active region or the current line
+and moves point to the first copy. A numeric prefix specifies the number of
+copies. <code>copy-from-above-command</code> copies text at the current column
+from the nearest nonblank line above; a numeric prefix limits the number of
+characters copied.</p>
+
